# CTIME(3)

## NAME

ctime, localtime, gmtime, asctime, timezone, clock, difftime, mktime, strftime - convert date
and time to ASCII

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <time.h>

char *ctime(const time_t *clock);

struct tm *localtime(const time_t *clock);

struct tm *gmtime(const time_t *clock);

char *asctime(const struct tm *tm);

char *timezone(int zone, int dst);

clock_t clock(void);

double difftime(time_t end, time_t start);

time_t mktime(struct tm *tp);

size_t strftime(char *s, size_t n, const char *fmt, const struct tm *tp);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Ctime* converts a time pointed to by *clock* such as returned by time(2) into ASCII and
returns a pointer to a 26-character string in the following form. All the fields have constant
width.

Sun Sep 16 01:03:52 1973\\n\\0

*Localtime* and *gmtime* return pointers to structures containing the broken-down time.
*Localtime* corrects for the time zone and possible daylight savings time; *gmtime* converts
directly to GMT, which is the time UNIX uses. *Asctime* converts a broken-down time to ASCII
and returns a pointer to a 26-character string.

The structure declaration from the include file is:

> ```c
> struct tm {
>     int tm_sec;
>     int tm_min;
>     int tm_hour;
>     int tm_mday;
>     int tm_mon;
>     int tm_year;
>     int tm_wday;
>     int tm_yday;
>     int tm_isdst;
> };
> ```

**Note:** v7's page pulled this in with a **.so** of the installed header; it is written out
here, there being no /usr/include on this system. <time.h> also declares every routine now.
v7's held the struct alone and left the calls to K&R's implicit **int**.

These quantities give the time on a 24-hour clock, day of month (1-31), month of year (0-11),
day of week (Sunday = 0), year - 1900, day of year (0-365), and a flag that is nonzero if
daylight saving time is in effect.

When local time is called for, the program consults the system to determine the time zone and
whether the standard U.S.A. daylight saving time adjustment is appropriate. The program knows
about the peculiarities of this conversion in 1974 and 1975; if necessary, a table for these
years can be extended. **Note:** the zone comes from ftime(2), as it did in v7 - the kernel
owns the westward offset and the daylight flag, and there is no zoneinfo to read.

*Timezone* returns the name of the time zone associated with its first argument, which is
measured in minutes westward from Greenwich. If the second argument is 0, the standard name is
used, otherwise the Daylight Saving version. If the required name does not appear in a table
built into the routine, the difference from GMT is produced; e.g. in Afghanistan
*timezone(-(60*4+30), 0)* is appropriate because it is 4:30 ahead of GMT and the string
**GMT+4:30** is produced. **Note:** *timezone* is declared by no header, C11 having **%Z**
instead; a caller that wants the v7 spelling declares it itself. So does *dysize*().

**Note:** the four that follow are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.

*Clock* returns the processor time used, in units of **CLOCKS_PER_SEC**, or (clock_t)-1 if it
is not available.

*Difftime* returns the difference of two times in seconds, as a **double**.

*Mktime* is the inverse of *localtime*: it takes a broken-down local time, normalises its
fields, fills in *tm_wday* and *tm_yday*, and returns the corresponding **time_t**, or
(time_t)-1 if it cannot be represented.

*Strftime* formats a broken-down time into *s* according to *fmt*, writing at most *n*
characters, and returns the number written not counting the terminating null - or 0 if the
result would not fit.

## SEE ALSO

time(2), ftime(2)

## BUGS

The return values point to static data whose content is overwritten by each call.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### time_t is one word, and it reaches past 2100

41 bits of seconds, so there is **no 2038 problem** - and that is not merely reassuring,
because it breaks a v7 assumption.

**dysize() uses the full Gregorian rule now.** v7 wrote **(y % 4) == 0** and was right until
2100, which a two-digit year could not reach and a 41-bit **time_t** can.

The same collapse is why stime(2) sheds an argument that v7's gate carried: v7's **time_t** was
two PDP-11 words.

### CLOCKS_PER_SEC is HZ

250, the tick this kernel programs. *Clock* can be no finer than the clock the machine has.

**Note:** under b6sim the counters are the *host's* clock ticks, whose rate is not this
machine's HZ. A program that divides by CLOCKS_PER_SEC gets a different answer in the two
harnesses, which is why the test converts only literal **time_t** values.

### Two more departures from v7's arithmetic

*Gmtime* and *asctime* **name their fields.** v7 walked **struct tm** through an **int \***,
filling it positionally. That works here only because every field is one word, and it is not
worth relying on.

**February is not patched in place.** v7 wrote 29 into its static month-length table for the
duration of a leap year and wrote 28 back afterwards - a static mutated and restored, which any
early return leaves wrong.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/ctime.c, clock.c, difftime.c, mktime.c, strftime.c, timezone.c
